      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the area of the shaded region. The figure consists of three semicircles: $ADB$, $BEC$, and $DFE$, all with a radius of 1 unit. The area calculation will involve subtracting the areas of the semicircles that are not part of the shaded region from the total area of the semicircles.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of one semicircle. The area of a semicircle with radius $r$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of a semicircle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi r^2\n\\]\nSince the radius $r$ is 1 unit, the area of one semicircle is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\pi (1)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi\n\\]\nThere are three semicircles in the figure, so the total area of the three semicircles is:\n\\[\n3 \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\pi = \\frac{3}{2} \\pi\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of the center semicircle $DFE$. The center semicircle $DFE$ is also a semicircle with radius 1 unit, so its area is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\pi (1)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi\n\\]\n\nThe shaded region consists of the areas of the three semicircles minus the area of the center semicircle. Therefore, the area of the shaded region is:\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{2} \\pi - \\frac{1}{2} \\pi = \\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of the shaded region is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]",
