      "text": "To solve the problem, let's first understand the geometry of the semicircles and the shaded region.\n\n1. **Identify the lengths of the arcs:**\n   - Each semicircle has a radius of 1 unit.\n   - The circumference of a full circle with radius 1 is \\(2\\pi \\times 1 = 2\\pi\\).\n   - Therefore, the length of each arc \\(ADB\\) and \\(BEC\\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n\n2. **Determine the coordinates of the points:**\n   - The points \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) are on the x-axis at \\((0,0)\\), \\((2,0)\\), and \\((4,0)\\) respectively.\n   - The point \\(D\\), being the midpoint of arc \\(ADB\\), is at \\((1,0)\\).\n   - The point \\(E\\), being the midpoint of arc \\(BEC\\), is at \\((3,0)\\).\n   - The point \\(F\\), being the midpoint of arc \\(DFE\\), is at \\((2,1)\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of the semicircle \\(DFE\\):**\n   - The arc \\(DFE\\) is a semicircle with a radius of \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n   - The area of a full circle with radius \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) is \\(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\).\n   - Therefore, the area of the semicircle \\(DFE\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\div 2 = \\frac{\\pi}{8}\\).\n\n4. **Calculate the area of the shaded region:**\n   - The shaded region is the area of the semicircle \\(DFE\\) minus the area of the two semicircles \\(ADB\\) and \\(BEC\\).\n   - The area of one semicircle \\(ADB\\) or \\(BEC\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\).\n   - Therefore, the total area of the two semicircles \\(ADB\\) and \\(BEC\\) is \\(2 \\times \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n   - The area of the shaded region is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{8} - \\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\frac{\\pi}{8} - \\frac{4\\pi}{8} = -\\frac{3\\pi}{8}\\).\n\nHowever, there seems to be a misunderstanding in the interpretation of the problem. The shaded region is actually the area of the semicircle \\(ABE\\) plus the area of the triangle \\(AFB\\).\n\n5. **Calculate the area of the semicircle \\(ABE\\):**\n   - The semicircle \\(ABE\\) has a radius of \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) (since \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) are the midpoints of the arcs, the distance \\(DE\\) is the diameter of the semicircle \\(ABE\\), which is equal to the sum of the radii of the two semicircles minus the radius of the larger semicircle).\n   - The area of a full circle with radius \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) is \\(\\pi (\\sqrt{3})^2 = 3\\pi\\).\n   - Therefore, the area of the semicircle \\(ABE\\) is \\(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\).\n\n6. **Calculate the area of the triangle \\(AFB\\):**\n   - The triangle \\(AFB\\) is a right triangle with legs of length 1 (since \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) are on the x-axis).\n   - The area of the triangle \\(AFB\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 \\times 1 = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n7. **Calculate the area of the shaded region:**\n   - The area of the shaded region is the area of the semicircle \\(ABE\\) minus the area of the triangle \\(AFB\\).\n   - The area of the shaded region is \\(\\frac{3\\pi}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3\\pi - 1}{2}\\).\n\nTherefore, the area of the shaded region is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3\\pi - 1}{2}}\\).",
